<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Defs>

  <!-- Watching the entity you keep caged, as recreation.

       THE STARTING OBSERVATION: the Anomaly DLC contains **no** recreation content at all. Not a
       `joyKind`, not a `JoyGiverDef`, not a `JoyKindDef` anywhere in the DLC - checked by a full
       sweep. You capture horrors, you bolt them to a platform in the middle of the base, you
       study them... and nobody ever looks at them again. That is the hole this mod fills.

       THE TYPE IS NEW, AND THAT IS THE POINT. The base game offers only ten recreation types, four
       of which come from a building; expectations ask for up to six different ones and tolerance
       is counted per type. An eleventh is therefore worth far more than a tenth building on a type
       already served - and this one costs nothing beyond a platform that was already built for
       study.

       THE VIEWING DISTANCE IS THE REAL SETTING, and it is deliberately short: 2 to 6 cells. A
       nociosphere carries a `CompProperties_CauseHediff_AoE` that applies `PainField` within a
       radius of 5.9. In other words, a good share of the audience will be standing INSIDE its pain
       field. That is not an oversight: the price of the show comes out of an existing vanilla
       mechanic, without a line of code, and it varies by entity - a fleshbeast hurts nobody.
       Widening the range is enough to make the activity harmless.

       NO THOUGHT IS GRANTED. It would cut both ways: a positive one would reward contemplating an
       abomination; a negative one would turn a source of recreation into a source of malus. The
       joy gained is already the reward, and the pain field already the price. It is one line to
       add if the wish comes.

       `MayRequire` RATHER THAN A GUARDED PATCH: the repository rule targets Workshop mods and
       their `_steam` suffix, which escapes `MayRequire`. Anomaly is a DLC, its packageId is fixed,
       and this is how the base game goes about it itself. -->

  <JoyKindDef MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Anomaly">
    <defName>EG_EntityGazing</defName>
    <label>entity gazing</label>
  </JoyKindDef>

  <JobDef MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Anomaly">
    <defName>EG_WatchEntity</defName>
    <!-- The vanilla driver, underived: a holding platform IS a `Building`, so there is nothing to
         retype - unlike the anima tree, which is a `Plant` and blew up the cast in
         `JobDriver_SitFacingBuilding`. -->
    <driverClass>JobDriver_WatchBuilding</driverClass>
    <reportString>watching the contained entity.</reportString>
    <joyDuration>4000</joyDuration>
    <joyMaxParticipants>5</joyMaxParticipants>
    <joyKind>EG_EntityGazing</joyKind>
    <allowOpportunisticPrefix>true</allowOpportunisticPrefix>
  </JobDef>

  <JoyGiverDef MayRequire="Ludeon.RimWorld.Anomaly">
    <defName>EG_WatchEntity</defName>
    <giverClass>EntityGazing.JoyGiver_WatchEntity</giverClass>
    <baseChance>2</baseChance>
    <!-- `desireSit` false: you do not pull up a chair in front of a cage of horrors, you stay on
         your feet, ready to leave. -->
    <desireSit>false</desireSit>
    <thingDefs>
      <li>HoldingPlatform</li>
    </thingDefs>
    <jobDef>EG_WatchEntity</jobDef>
    <joyKind>EG_EntityGazing</joyKind>
    <!-- Sight alone. A blind colonist has no business here, and hands are of no use: you very
         much do not touch. -->
    <requiredCapacities>
      <li>Sight</li>
    </requiredCapacities>
  </JoyGiverDef>

</Defs>
